title: 'presentation chains are like cons-lists in lisp : lengthy explanation'
pid: 987bb1be-4c61-4bea-b749-00feb3e8845b
body: |2-
  Recall that a cons-list is represented (a, (b, (c, ...))).

  Presentation can proceed within a presented atom.

  I have represented this the following imperfect way:
  - A
  -- link to B
  --- link to C
  - B
  -- C
  -- D
  Here B presents C and D,
  and A presents not just B but the fact that B presents C.

  What I am saying in the A tree is 'B is relevant to A,
  and among what B presents, C is particularly relevant to A --
  relevant enough to present it here too.'
  The user can of course visit B to see,
  in addition to C, everything else B presents.

  It would be more precise to represent the latter as
  (A presents (B presents C)).
